title: Storage open questions
tags: Storage
kind: question
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: What the storage work of November 2026 left to look at again — replace and rename as ordinary operations, what a record keeps of a mass write, and compressing what the log keeps of a deletion.

Raised while building the tests of undo and redo ([[The operation log]], `tests/undo_redo_properties.rs`) and the change of the value partition to postings ([[Sorting and postings]]). None is decided.

* ''Replacing a value and renaming a field are not mass writes.'' `PATCH /metarecords/:uuid/fields/:name` and `POST …/fields/:name/rename` write one row at a time (`replace_owned_row`), and so does `retype_field`: a replacement of a value on every match of a query is as many operations as records, in the log and in the store. The batch endpoint (`query/fields/batch`) only runs the four mass writes in one transaction. To see again: whether a replacement (a remove then an add) and a rename (an unset then a set) should be one stub each, or composed from the mass writes, and what each reads as in the log.
* ''What a record keeps of the mass writes it was in.'' A record written by hand after a mass write carries the number of the operation that wrote each of its rows, and, for what was taken off it, a ''tomb'' (a value taken off after the entries older than the number) or a ''cleared'' field (emptied after them) — doc "Mass writes in the store". Deleting the record deletes its key, so these marks go with it, and the log records its rows and not them. A record given back is written from its rows only, so an entry older than the deletion that is still pending may add back what the record had lost. To see again with the keeping of the dense id of a deleted record ([[Reverting onto the current state]]).
* ''Compressing what the log keeps of a deletion'' (user, 17 November 2026). A deletion keeps the rows of its record uncompressed, one copy in the snapshots; the stubs of the mass writes keep their before in a compressed form of their own (nothing for an add or a remove, places for a replacement). A block of consecutive records of a mass deletion, compressed with a general algorithm, may be cheap enough to write the rows when the deletion is made, which would remove the dependence of a pending deletion given back on the state of its records, which a mark and a copy at the first write settle without writing the rows at the deletion ([[Mass operations in the log]]). Not measured. To see: the ratio on a real repository, the cost of reading one record of a block, and whether the values of `records` and `snaps` should be compressed whatever the table.
